WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.512
A security anti-pattern is a repeated engineering shortcut that appears useful in the moment but creates predictable risk later.

2
00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:16.093
It is different from a one-time bug because the same pattern tends to show up across features, teams, or projects.

3
00:00:16.093 --> 00:00:21.147
It becomes a habit, a copied example, or a default way of solving a problem.

4
00:00:21.147 --> 00:00:25.868
Anti-patterns often survive because they solve a local problem quickly.

5
00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:27.198
A test passes.

6
00:00:27.198 --> 00:00:28.528
A demo works.

7
00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.457
A browser warning disappears.

8
00:00:30.457 --> 00:00:32.319
A deployment becomes easier.

9
00:00:32.319 --> 00:00:33.782
A support case closes.

10
00:00:33.782 --> 00:00:44.821
The shortcut feels productive because it removes friction from the immediate task, but it also removes a control, hides an assumption, or shifts risk into production.

11
00:00:44.821 --> 00:00:54.264
The risk appears when the shortcut crosses a trust boundary, scales to more users, reaches sensitive data, or gets copied into another system.

12
00:00:54.264 --> 00:00:58.453
A local development credential becomes a production credential.

13
00:00:58.453 --> 00:01:01.712
A hidden UI button becomes the only access check.

14
00:01:01.712 --> 00:01:06.965
A domain model returned from one endpoint becomes a pattern for every endpoint.

15
00:01:06.965 --> 00:01:09.692
Anti-patterns are not only code problems.

16
00:01:09.692 --> 00:01:19.999
They can live in configuration, deployment scripts, logs, dashboards, support workflows, CI output, documentation, sample projects, and team review habits.

17
00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:27.447
A C# application can have well-written methods and still inherit unsafe behavior from the environment around it.

18
00:01:27.447 --> 00:01:34.696
The goal of this course is to recognize recurring patterns and replace them with safer engineering practices.

19
00:01:34.696 --> 00:01:37.023
A good replacement is not a slogan.

20
00:01:37.023 --> 00:01:50.789
It is reviewable: clear ownership, safe defaults, controlled configuration, validated inputs, server-side authorization, shaped outputs, redacted diagnostics, and production behavior that matches the design.
